# GitHub Actions

## Linking Conventions

When referencing GitHub Actions in workflows, docs, or code:

- **Link to the repo or action** — when it's elegant and subtle, make the action name or version a link
- **Link to the specific version** — when pinning (e.g. `@v1` or `@1.4.1`), link the version to the release/tag URL when possible
- **Prefer subtle over noisy** — a single link in a footer or a small inline reference is better than repeated links

## Examples

- **Release footer:** `Created by [creo-team/action-release](repo)@[1.4.1](releases/tag/1.4.1)` — repo link + version link
- **Workflow comment:** `# Uses creo-team/action-release — see https://github.com/creo-team/action-release`
- **README:** `[creo-team/action-release@v1](https://github.com/creo-team/action-release)` — action ref as link

## When to Link

- Release notes, changelogs, and footers
- README or docs that reference workflows
- When the action is a key dependency

## When to Skip

- Inline workflows where the link would clutter
- When the reference is already in a linked block
